1
00:00:00,094 --> 00:00:04,511
In 1995, a movie based on a video
game did something nobody expected.

2
00:00:04,832 --> 00:00:06,008
It opened at number one.

3
00:00:06,367 --> 00:00:08,814
It stayed at number one for
three straight weekends.

4
00:00:09,174 --> 00:00:12,084
It earned more than
$120 million worldwide.

5
00:00:12,431 --> 00:00:14,655
And the man at the center
of it wasn't just the star.

6
00:00:15,004 --> 00:00:17,059
He was also quietly fixing the fights.

7
00:00:17,421 --> 00:00:21,194
His name is Robin Shou, and the story
people tell about what happened next

8
00:00:21,194 --> 00:00:23,211
is wrong. They say he disappeared.

9
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:26,001
They say he went home to
Hong Kong and faded away.

10
00:00:26,317 --> 00:00:26,932
He didn't.

11
00:00:27,268 --> 00:00:28,680
He stayed in Los Angeles.

12
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:29,685
He waited.

13
00:00:30,029 --> 00:00:33,217
And the industry that had just made
a fortune off his face and his fists

14
00:00:33,217 --> 00:00:34,729
had almost nothing to offer him.

15
00:00:35,085 --> 00:00:36,690
This is the story of Robin Shou.

16
00:00:37,042 --> 00:00:40,162
Robin Shou was born in British
Hong Kong in nineteen sixty.

17
00:00:40,472 --> 00:00:43,700
In nineteen seventy-one, his
family moved to Los Angeles.

18
00:00:44,053 --> 00:00:47,414
A small apartment, a new
language, a new country.

19
00:00:47,724 --> 00:00:50,739
He grew up an American kid in a
neighborhood that was still figuring

20
00:00:50,739 --> 00:00:53,905
out what it was, and he did what
a good son was supposed to do.

21
00:00:54,263 --> 00:00:56,004
He went to Cal State Los Angeles.

22
00:00:56,364 --> 00:00:57,888
He studied civil engineering.

23
00:00:58,251 --> 00:01:00,227
A stable job was waiting at the end of it.

24
00:01:00,562 --> 00:01:02,004
Martial arts wasn't the plan.

25
00:01:02,366 --> 00:01:04,917
He tried karate for a
short time and dropped it.

26
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:08,434
Then, around nineteen, he saw
a demonstration of Chinese

27
00:01:08,438 --> 00:01:10,249
Wushu and something clicked.

28
00:01:10,592 --> 00:01:13,161
He has described that moment
as a kind of recognition.

29
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:14,509
This is Chinese.

30
00:01:14,847 --> 00:01:15,603
This is mine.

31
00:01:15,949 --> 00:01:18,312
Just before his senior
year, he sold his car.

32
00:01:18,665 --> 00:01:22,711
He used the money to fly to China and
train Wushu for a full academic quarter.

33
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:25,854
His parents reportedly found
out where he was only when word

34
00:01:25,855 --> 00:01:27,064
reached them through the family.

35
00:01:27,385 --> 00:01:29,339
It was the first big gamble of his life.

36
00:01:29,675 --> 00:01:30,828
It would not be the last.

37
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:34,872
He came back with medals and with a
reputation in the martial arts world.

38
00:01:35,208 --> 00:01:36,530
But medals don't pay rent.

39
00:01:36,874 --> 00:01:39,720
After graduation, he took
a job as a soil engineer.

40
00:01:40,068 --> 00:01:44,851
Eighteen months, a desk, a screen,
and a slow creeping feeling that

41
00:01:44,851 --> 00:01:46,492
he was living someone else's life.

42
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:49,426
In the late nineteen eighties,
he flew to Hong Kong.

43
00:01:49,804 --> 00:01:51,464
Officially, it was a vacation.

44
00:01:51,811 --> 00:01:53,173
Really, it was an escape.

45
00:01:53,517 --> 00:01:56,801
One afternoon, while he was training
at a gym, someone asked if he

46
00:01:56,801 --> 00:01:58,490
wanted to do stunt work on a film.

47
00:01:58,836 --> 00:01:59,845
It was a small job.

48
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:00,887
Take a hit.

49
00:02:01,229 --> 00:02:01,875
Hit a wall.

50
00:02:02,202 --> 00:02:02,978
Don't complain.

51
00:02:03,327 --> 00:02:05,999
He could do all of that,
and the stunt team noticed.

52
00:02:06,323 --> 00:02:09,455
One job became another, and
suddenly there was no reason to

53
00:02:09,455 --> 00:02:11,738
go back to the desk, so he stayed.

54
00:02:12,054 --> 00:02:12,780
He lived cheap.

55
00:02:13,122 --> 00:02:16,068
A rooftop apartment, a tiny
rent, a lot of ambition.

56
00:02:16,435 --> 00:02:18,786
And the early roles were
exactly what you'd expect.

57
00:02:19,137 --> 00:02:21,577
Henchman, bodyguard, assassin.

58
00:02:21,908 --> 00:02:23,640
No name, no lines.

59
00:02:23,993 --> 00:02:26,205
Thirty seconds on screen
and then the floor.

60
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:29,711
But he kept showing up, and
slowly, the parts got bigger.

61
00:02:30,066 --> 00:02:33,921
Over the next several years, he appeared
in dozens of Hong Kong action films.

62
00:02:34,274 --> 00:02:36,866
He worked alongside some of
the biggest names of the era.

63
00:02:37,217 --> 00:02:40,683
In Tiger Cage II, he played a
villain opposite Donnie Yen in a

64
00:02:40,683 --> 00:02:44,547
film directed by Yuen Woo-ping, the
same Yuen Woo-ping who would later

65
00:02:44,547 --> 00:02:46,174
design the fights for The Matrix.

66
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:49,908
He shared screens with stars like
Simon Yam and Cynthia Rothrock.

67
00:02:50,245 --> 00:02:51,218
He was respected.

68
00:02:51,530 --> 00:02:52,337
He was working.

69
00:02:52,678 --> 00:02:56,690
But by his own account, his films
were medium hits, not big hits.

70
00:02:57,041 --> 00:03:00,374
He was a solid name in a crowded
field, and after years of it,

71
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:01,851
the thrill started to fade.

72
00:03:02,178 --> 00:03:04,969
By nineteen ninety-four,
he was back in Los Angeles.

73
00:03:05,326 --> 00:03:08,082
He was thinking about starting
an import-export business.

74
00:03:08,441 --> 00:03:10,943
He was, in his own mind,
finished with acting.

75
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:12,389
Then his agent called.

76
00:03:12,733 --> 00:03:13,531
There was a movie.

77
00:03:13,833 --> 00:03:15,268
It was based on a video game.

78
00:03:15,606 --> 00:03:17,019
It was called Mortal Kombat.

79
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:18,169
Robin laughed.

80
00:03:18,511 --> 00:03:21,366
It sounded like the dumbest
name he had ever heard, and

81
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:22,822
he knew how this usually went.

82
00:03:23,155 --> 00:03:27,632
An Asian actor in a big Hollywood movie
meant one thing, the villain, menacing for

83
00:03:27,632 --> 00:03:30,041
ten minutes, dead before the third act.

84
00:03:30,386 --> 00:03:31,344
That was the default.

85
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:32,613
That was the ceiling.

86
00:03:32,949 --> 00:03:35,289
His agent pushed, "Just read the script."

87
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:39,196
So he did, and he found out the
role of Liu Kang wasn't a henchman.

88
00:03:39,546 --> 00:03:40,452
It wasn't the villain.

89
00:03:40,812 --> 00:03:45,457
It was the hero, an Asian hero driving the
story from the first scene to the last.

90
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:46,869
That changed everything.

91
00:03:47,216 --> 00:03:49,133
But getting it was another fight entirely.

92
00:03:49,433 --> 00:03:53,164
By multiple accounts, he went through
an extraordinary number of auditions,

93
00:03:53,492 --> 00:03:59,259
reportedly seven, for the producers, for
the director, for the studio, because the

94
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,050
people writing the checks were nervous.

95
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,629
A big budget action
movie with an Asian lead.

96
00:04:03,964 --> 00:04:06,542
In 1994, that was considered a risk.

97
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:07,978
He got the part anyway.

98
00:04:08,304 --> 00:04:10,076
The director was Paul W. S. Anderson.

99
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:14,102
It was only his second feature film,
and he had very little

100
00:04:14,102 --> 00:04:15,845
experience shooting martial arts.

101
00:04:16,219 --> 00:04:19,183
Early on, fights were being
shot in wide, simple takes.

102
00:04:19,519 --> 00:04:21,349
One angle, no coverage.

103
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:24,510
Robin, who had spent nearly
a decade inside Hong Kong

104
00:04:24,519 --> 00:04:26,022
action, knew what was missing.

105
00:04:26,377 --> 00:04:28,190
Martial arts on screen is about rhythm.

106
00:04:28,537 --> 00:04:30,205
It's about cutting to the right moment.

107
00:04:30,543 --> 00:04:33,178
It's about showing the hit
and the reaction to the hit.

108
00:04:33,532 --> 00:04:37,678
After test screenings, audiences said
the same thing, "We want more fighting."

109
00:04:38,031 --> 00:04:41,430
So Robin helped create more,
including the fight fans still talk

110
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,609
about, Liu Kang versus Reptile.

111
00:04:43,969 --> 00:04:46,179
Anderson later gave him
credit for that work.

112
00:04:46,518 --> 00:04:49,582
Those scenes were among the first
times Hollywood used Hong Kong-style

113
00:04:49,582 --> 00:04:53,398
wirework at this scale, years
before The Matrix, and Robin had

114
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:55,107
his own system for rating a fight.

115
00:04:55,438 --> 00:04:56,813
How many ribs he bruised.

116
00:04:57,154 --> 00:04:59,831
"The Reptile fight," he said,
"was a three-rib fight."

117
00:05:00,162 --> 00:05:03,506
Mortal Kombat opened on August 18th, 1995.

118
00:05:03,851 --> 00:05:06,241
$23 million on opening weekend.

119
00:05:06,584 --> 00:05:08,514
Number one for three weekends in a row.

120
00:05:08,866 --> 00:05:12,774
More than 70 million at home,
roughly 50 million more overseas,

121
00:05:12,858 --> 00:05:17,442
for about 122 million worldwide,
on a budget around 20 million.

122
00:05:17,805 --> 00:05:21,889
It became the highest grossing video game
movie ever made at that point, a record

123
00:05:21,889 --> 00:05:23,585
that stood until later hits took it.

124
00:05:23,897 --> 00:05:26,236
Robin Shou was now the
lead of a worldwide hit.

125
00:05:26,578 --> 00:05:28,400
The sequel should have
been his victory lap.

126
00:05:28,741 --> 00:05:30,283
Instead, it was a funeral.

127
00:05:30,629 --> 00:05:33,698
Mortal Kombat: Annihilation
arrived in 1997.

128
00:05:34,049 --> 00:05:38,082
A bigger budget, almost none of the
discipline that made the first film work.

129
00:05:38,408 --> 00:05:42,154
It earned barely 50 million worldwide,
less than half of the original.

130
00:05:42,498 --> 00:05:43,474
Critics buried it.

131
00:05:43,806 --> 00:05:46,400
Its Rotten Tomatoes score
was in the single digits.

132
00:05:46,736 --> 00:05:49,604
Even people close to the franchise
have called it a low point.

133
00:05:49,909 --> 00:05:50,897
Robin returned.

134
00:05:51,233 --> 00:05:53,001
Most of the original cast did not.

135
00:05:53,347 --> 00:05:55,650
A third film was planned,
and then it was dead.

136
00:05:55,981 --> 00:05:59,169
It sat in development limbo
for years, and in the end, the

137
00:05:59,169 --> 00:06:00,980
franchise was rebooted without him.

138
00:06:01,335 --> 00:06:02,712
Here is the part that matters.

139
00:06:03,073 --> 00:06:06,600
This was an actor who had headlined
a successful franchise, who had

140
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:10,482
shaped some of its best action, who
had proved he could open a film, and

141
00:06:10,482 --> 00:06:11,955
the offers that followed were small.

142
00:06:12,304 --> 00:06:13,302
Shockingly small.
